# Parity with PyVO

```elixir
Mix.install([
   {:space_tap, path: "/Users/jaime/code/space_tap"},
  {:pythonx, "~> 0.4.11"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19.1"}
])
```

```pyproject.toml
[project]
name = "pyvo_parity"
version = "0.0.0"
requires-python = "==3.13.*"
dependencies = [
  "astropy==8.0.2",
  "pyvo==1.9.1"
]
```

## What this notebook compares

[PyVO](https://pyvo.readthedocs.io/) is an Astropy-affiliated Python client for IVOA services, and SpaceTAP uses it as a reference implementation: its parity tests run the same query through both clients against a live service and compare the results. This notebook makes those comparisons in the open, with the query in an Elixir cell and PyVO in a Python cell next to it.

PyVO asks for no format, and when a query names neither `FORMAT` nor `RESPONSEFORMAT` the service sends VOTable (TAP 1.1 §2.7.3), which PyVO reads with a type for each column. SpaceTAP asks for CSV by default, whose values are all strings, and for VOTable with `format: :votable`. So, like the parity tests, the comparisons here ask SpaceTAP for VOTable and check what both clients read from it: the column names, whether the service cut the result short, and the value of every cell.

Python cells need Livebook 0.16 or later. The setup installs [Pythonx](https://hexdocs.pm/pythonx), which runs a Python interpreter inside the Elixir runtime ([Embedding Python in Elixir, it's Fine](https://dashbit.co/blog/running-python-in-elixir-its-fine) explains how). The `pyproject.toml` cell under it tells [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) what to install: Python 3.13, PyVO 1.9.1 and astropy 8.0.2, the versions the parity tests pin, with their dependencies, such as numpy. The first run downloads all of it and takes a while; later runs use uv's cache.

## How the two languages share variables

A Python cell sees the Elixir variables it names, and the variables it assigns come back to Elixir. Pythonx converts values on the way:

* An Elixir string arrives as `bytes`, not `str`, because an Elixir binary doesn't have to be valid UTF-8. Python code calls `.decode()` on it.
* What Python assigns comes back as a `%Pythonx.Object{}`. `Pythonx.decode/1` turns the built-in Python types (`None`, booleans, numbers, strings, lists, tuples, dicts and sets) into Elixir terms. Anything else, such as a PyVO result or a numpy number, stays an object.

```elixir
greeting = "Hello from Elixir"
```

```python
shout = greeting.decode().upper()
type(greeting), type(shout)
```

```elixir
{shout, Pythonx.decode(shout)}
```

## Helpers

`pyvo_query` runs a query with PyVO, and `pyvo_result` turns what it returns into built-in Python types, which `Pythonx.decode/1` can bring to Elixir. The functions are Python objects too, so they come back to Elixir like any other variable, and the Python cells below that call them get them from there.

`to_table()` gives the table as astropy reads it, and `tolist()` turns each column into Python values, with `None` for a masked cell, which is how astropy marks a null. `value` applies the rest of SpaceTAP's rules ([ADR 0007](https://github.com/jaimeiniesta/space_tap/blob/main/docs/adr/0007-how-votable-values-map-to-elixir.md)) where they differ from astropy's: NaN and an empty string or array are null too. Elixir has no float for infinity, so SpaceTAP gives `:infinity` and `:neg_infinity`, and `value` writes them as strings:

```python
import math

import pyvo


def pyvo_query(service_url, adql, maxrec=None):
    service = pyvo.dal.TAPService(service_url.decode())
    return service.search(adql.decode(), maxrec=maxrec)


def pyvo_result(result):
    columns = [column.tolist() for column in result.to_table().columns.values()]

    return {
        "columns": list(result.fieldnames),
        "rows": [[value(cell) for cell in row] for row in zip(*columns)],
        "overflow": result.query_status == "OVERFLOW",
    }


def value(cell):
    if hasattr(cell, "tolist"):
        cell = cell.tolist()

    if isinstance(cell, list):
        return [value(item) for item in cell] or None
    if isinstance(cell, float) and math.isnan(cell):
        return None
    if isinstance(cell, float) and math.isinf(cell):
        return "Infinity" if cell > 0 else "-Infinity"
    if cell == "":
        return None
    return cell
```

`Parity.compare/2` makes the comparison of the parity tests, which live in [`test/support/pyvo.ex`](https://github.com/jaimeiniesta/space_tap/blob/main/test/support/pyvo.ex). Without `ORDER BY` the rows may come in any order (ADQL 2.1 §2.2), so they are compared sorted, and with `===`, so `1` and `1.0` differ:

```elixir
defmodule Parity do
  def compare(%SpaceTAP.Result{} = result, pyvo) do
    %{"columns" => columns, "rows" => rows, "overflow" => overflow} = Pythonx.decode(pyvo)
    spacetap_rows = Enum.map(result.rows, fn row -> Enum.map(row, &comparable/1) end)

    %{
      same_columns?: result.columns == columns,
      same_overflow?: result.overflow == overflow,
      same_values?: Enum.sort(spacetap_rows) === Enum.sort(rows),
      rows: %{spacetap: length(result.rows), pyvo: length(rows)}
    }
  end

  defp comparable(:infinity), do: "Infinity"
  defp comparable(:neg_infinity), do: "-Infinity"
  defp comparable(values) when is_list(values), do: Enum.map(values, &comparable/1)
  defp comparable(value), do: value
end
```

## The Pleiades on GAVO

The five brightest stars within one degree of the centre of the Pleiades, from `gaia.dr3lite`, the copy of Gaia DR3 at the GAVO Data Center, the German Virtual Observatory's archive. The service URL and the query are Elixir variables, defined once and used by both clients:

```elixir
gavo = "https://dc.g-vo.org/tap"

adql = """
SELECT TOP 5 source_id, ra, dec, parallax, phot_g_mean_mag
FROM gaia.dr3lite
WHERE CONTAINS(POINT('ICRS', ra, dec), CIRCLE('ICRS', 56.75, 24.12, 1.0)) = 1
  AND parallax IS NOT NULL
  AND phot_g_mean_mag IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY phot_g_mean_mag
"""

{:ok, pleiades} = SpaceTAP.query(gavo, adql, format: :votable)
pleiades
```

PyVO returns a `TAPResults`:

```python
pyvo_pleiades = pyvo_query(gavo, adql)
pleiades_from_pyvo = pyvo_result(pyvo_pleiades)
pyvo_pleiades
```

```elixir
Parity.compare(pleiades, pleiades_from_pyvo)
```

## The same row three ways

This is the first row as SpaceTAP reads it from CSV and from VOTable, and as PyVO reads it, next to the `datatype` that each `FIELD` of the VOTable declares. A numpy number becomes a plain Python number first, which `Pythonx.decode/1` can turn into an Elixir one:

```python
import numpy

pyvo_row = pyvo_pleiades[0]


def plain(value):
    return value.item() if isinstance(value, numpy.generic) else value


pyvo_fields = [
    (field.name, field.datatype, type(pyvo_row[field.name]).__name__, plain(pyvo_row[field.name]))
    for field in pyvo_pleiades.fielddescs
]
```

```elixir
{:ok, %SpaceTAP.Result{rows: [csv_row | _]}} = SpaceTAP.query(gavo, adql)
[votable_row | _] = pleiades.rows

[Pythonx.decode(pyvo_fields), csv_row, votable_row]
|> Enum.zip_with(fn [{name, datatype, pyvo_type, pyvo_value}, csv, votable] ->
  %{
    "column" => name,
    "datatype" => datatype,
    "CSV" => inspect(csv),
    "VOTable" => inspect(votable),
    "PyVO type" => pyvo_type,
    "PyVO value in Elixir" => inspect(pyvo_value)
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(
  name: "First row",
  keys: ["column", "datatype", "CSV", "VOTable", "PyVO type", "PyVO value in Elixir"]
)
```

From CSV every value is the text of a cell. From VOTable, SpaceTAP and PyVO give each one the type its `FIELD` declares (VOTable 1.4 §6):

* `source_id` is a `long`, a 64-bit integer. PyVO reads a `numpy.int64` and SpaceTAP an integer.
* `ra` and `dec` are `double`, 64-bit floats like Elixir's.
* `parallax` and `phot_g_mean_mag` are `float`, 32-bit floats. PyVO keeps a `numpy.float32`, and SpaceTAP gives the exact value of its 32 bits as an Elixir float, whose floats all have 64 bits: `3.6157946586608887` where the CSV says `3.6157947`. Both are the same 32-bit number, and the numpy one turns into that same 64-bit value, so the comparison needs no tolerance.

## MAXREC on GAVO

`MAXREC` caps the number of rows the service sends (DALI 1.1 §3.4.4). The query asks for ten rows and `maxrec`, another Elixir variable both clients use, lets three through. `ORDER BY` makes both clients get the same three:

```elixir
adql = "SELECT TOP 10 source_id FROM gaia.dr3lite ORDER BY source_id"
maxrec = 3

{:ok, truncated} = SpaceTAP.query(gavo, adql, maxrec: maxrec, format: :votable)
truncated.overflow
```

```python
pyvo_truncated = pyvo_query(gavo, adql, maxrec=maxrec)
truncated_from_pyvo = pyvo_result(pyvo_truncated)
pyvo_truncated.query_status
```

```elixir
Parity.compare(truncated, truncated_from_pyvo)
```

Both get four rows: GAVO sends one more than `MAXREC` asks for, although DALI 1.1 §3.4.4 says the service "must only return the requested number of rows", and PyVO warns about it. Both know there were more. When `MAXREC` cuts a result, the service says so in the VOTable with `<INFO name="QUERY_STATUS" value="OVERFLOW"/>` (DALI 1.1 §4.4.1). PyVO shows it as `query_status`, and SpaceTAP sets `overflow`. CSV has nowhere to put it, so a CSV result has `overflow: nil`.

## The NASA Exoplanet Archive

The archive applies `TOP` before `ORDER BY`, so the query names its planets, and `default_flag = 1` picks one row of parameters for each:

```elixir
exoplanet_archive = "https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/TAP"

adql = """
SELECT pl_name, disc_year FROM ps
WHERE default_flag = 1 AND pl_name IN ('WASP-39 b', 'GJ 436 b', 'HAT-P-1 b')
"""

{:ok, planets} = SpaceTAP.query(exoplanet_archive, adql, format: :votable)
planets
```

```python
pyvo_planets = pyvo_query(exoplanet_archive, adql)
planets_from_pyvo = pyvo_result(pyvo_planets)
pyvo_planets
```

```elixir
Parity.compare(planets, planets_from_pyvo)
```

`disc_year` is an `int` in the VOTable, a 32-bit integer, so both clients read `2011` where SpaceTAP's CSV gives `"2011"`. The archive labels its CSV `text/plain`, and SpaceTAP reads it as CSV all the same ([ADR 0003](https://github.com/jaimeiniesta/space_tap/blob/main/docs/adr/0003-csv-sent-as-plain-text-is-read-as-csv.md)).

## The parity tests

The parity tests make these comparisons without Livebook. `test/support/pyvo.ex` calls a script that runs the query with PyVO through `uv run` and prints the column names, rows and overflow as JSON, with the rules of `value` above. They also ask SpaceTAP for CSV and check that it has the same columns and as many rows. Those for GAVO run with `mix test --only pyvo` and before every release, and those for every service in the README's Compatibility table with `mix test.services`.
